@style quote
[italic: XANTHIAS]

@style verse
  Shall I crack any of those old jokes, master,
  At which the audience never fail to laugh?

@style verse
  [strong: DIONYSUS.] Aye, what you will, except [italic: I'm getting crushed:] Fight shy of that: I'm sick of that already.

[strong: XAN.] Nothing else smart?

[strong: DIO.] Aye, save [italic: my shoulder's aching.]

[strong: XAN.] Come now, that comical joke?

@style verse
  [strong: DIO.] With all my heart. Only be careful not to shift your pole,
  And—

[strong: XAN.] What?

[strong: DIO.] And vow that you've a bellyache.

@style verse
  [strong: XAN.] May I not say I'm overburdened so
  That if none ease me, I must ease myself?

[strong: DIO.] For mercy's sake, not till I'm going to vomit.

[strong: XAN.]

@style verse
  What! must I bear these burdens, and not make
  One of the jokes Ameipsias and Lycis
  And Phrynichus, in every play they write,
  Put in the mouths of all their burden-bearers?

[strong: DIO.]

@style verse
  Don't make them; no! I tell you when I see
  Their plays, and hear those jokes, I come away
  More than a twelvemonth older than I went.

[strong: XAN.]

@style verse
  O thrice unlucky neck of mine, which now
  Is [italic: getting crushed], yet must not crack its joke!

[strong: DIO.]

@style verse
  Now is not this fine pampered insolence
  When I myself, Dionysus, son of—Pipkin,
  Toil on afoot, and let this fellow ride,
  Taking no trouble, and no burden bearing?

[strong: XAN.] What, don't I bear?

[strong: DIO.] How can you when you're riding?

[strong: XAN.] Why, I bear these.

[strong: DIO.] How?

[strong: XAN.] Most unwillingly.

[strong: DIO.] Does not the donkey bear the load you're bearing?

[strong: XAN.] Not what I bear myself: by Zeus, not he.

[strong: DIO.] How can you bear, when you are borne yourself?

[strong: XAN.] Don't know: but anyhow [italic: my shoulder's aching].

[strong: DIO.]

@style verse
  Then since you say the donkey helps you not,
  You lift him up and carry him in turn.

[strong: XAN.]

@style verse
  O hang it all! why didn't I fight at sea?
  You should have smarted bitterly for this.

[strong: DIO.]

@style verse
  Get down, you rascal; I've been trudging on
  Till now I've reached the portal, where I'm going
  First to turn in.
  Boy! Boy! I say there, Boy!

[strong: HERACLES.]

@style verse
  Who banged the door? How like a prancing Centaur
  He drove against it! Mercy o' me, what's this?

[strong: DIO.] Boy.

[strong: XAN.] Yes.

[strong: DIO.] Did you observe?

[strong: XAN.] What?

[strong: DIO.] How alarmed He is.

[strong: XAN.] Aye truly, lest you've lost your wits.

@style verse
  [strong: HER.] O by Demeter, I can't choose but laugh.
  Biting my lips won't stop me. Ha! ha! ha!

[strong: DIO.] Pray you, come hither, I have need of you.

@style verse
  [strong: HER.] I vow I can't help laughing, I can't help it.
  A lion's hide upon a yellow silk, a club and buskin!
  What's it all about? Where were you going?

[strong: DIO.] I was serving lately aboard the—Cleisthenes.

[strong: HER.] And fought?

[strong: DIO.] And sank more than a dozen of the enemy's ships.

[strong: HER.] You two?

[strong: DIO.] We two.

[strong: HER.] And then I awoke, and lo!

@style verse
  [strong: DIO.] There as, on deck, I'm reading to myself
