[strong: AEAC.] That's jolly too.

[strong: XAN.] How about prying?

[strong: AEAC.] That beats everything!

@style verse
  [strong: XAN.] Great Kin-god Zeus! And what of overhearing
  Your master's secrets?

[strong: AEAC.] What? I'm mad with joy.

[strong: XAN.] And blabbing them abroad?

[strong: AEAC.] O heaven and earth! When I do that, I can't contain myself.

@style verse
  [strong: XAN.] Phoebus Apollo! clap your hand in mine, Kiss and be kissed: and prithee tell me this, Tell me by Zeus, our rascaldom's own god, What's all that noise within? What means this hubbub And row?

[strong: AEAC.] That's Aeschylus and Euripides.

[strong: XAN.] Eh?

@style verse
  [strong: AEAC.] Wonderful, wonderful things are going on. The dead are rioting, taking different sides.

[strong: XAN.] Why, what's the matter?

@style verse
  [strong: AEAC.] There's a custom here
  With all the crafts, the good and noble crafts,
  That the chief master of his art in each
  Shall have his dinner in the assembly hall,
  And sit by Pluto's side.

[strong: XAN.] I understand.

@style verse
  [strong: AEAC.] Until another comes, more wise than he
  In the same art: then must the first give way.

[strong: XAN.] And how has this disturbed our Aeschylus?

@style verse
  [strong: AEAC.] 'Twas he that occupied the tragic chair,
  As, in his craft, the noblest.

[strong: XAN.] Who does now?

@style verse
  [strong: AEAC.] But when Euripides came down, he kept
  Flourishing off before the highwaymen,
  Thieves, burglars, parricides—these form our mob
  In Hades—till with listening to his twists
